nsportation And Safety"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20060010432,"ear":"NO","id":"361b33009ca145acbe4dbc3ee1640434","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-17T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","title":"GO/NO-GO - When is medical hazard mitigation acceptable for launch?","stiType":"ABSTRACT","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-08-23T16:34:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20060010432,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Hamilton, Douglas R."},"organization":{"name":"Wyle Labs., Inc.","location":"Houston, TX, United States"}},"id":"dc4d43ebb50649ba8c586ceef04d22fd"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20060010432,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Polk, James D."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Johnson Space Center","location":"Houston, TX, United States"}},"id":"fb93ab042eac42a9ab1fd8b9fc09df67"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Abstract","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20060010432,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2013-08-23T16:34:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"JSC","name":"Johnson Space Center","id":"dd46994b91a94cb2a2722617cbbadc55"},"onlyAbstract":true,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Medical support of spaceflight missions is composed of complex tasks and decisions that dedicated to maintaining the health and performance of the crew and the completion of mission objectives. Spacecraft represent one of the most complex vehicles built by humans, and are built to very rigorous design specifications. In the course of a Flight Readiness Review (FRR) or a mission itself, the flight surgeon must be able to understand the impact of hazards and risks that may not be completely mitigated by design alone. Some hazards are not mitigated because they are never actually identified. When a hazard is identified, it must be reduced or waivered. Hazards that cannot be designed out of the vehicle or mission, are usually mitigated through other means to bring the residual risk to an acceptable level. This is possible in most engineered systems because failure modes are usually predictable and analysis can include taking these systems to failure. Medical support of space missions is complicated by the inability of flight surgeons to provide \"exact\" hazard and risk numbers to the NASA engineering community. Taking humans to failure is not an option. Furthermore, medical dogma is mostly comprised of \"medical prevention\" strategies that mitigate risk by examining the behaviour of a cohort of humans similar to astronauts. Unfortunately, this approach does not lend itself well for predicting the effect of a hazard in the unique environment of space. This presentation will discuss how Medical Operations uses an evidence-based approach to decide if hazard mitigation strategies are adequate to reduce mission risk to acceptable levels. Case studies to be discussed will include: 1. Risk of electrocution risk during EVA 2. Risk of cardiac event risk during long and short duration missions 3. Degraded cabin environmental monitoring on the ISS. Learning Objectives 1.) The audience will understand the challenges of mitigating medical risk caused by nominal and off-nominal mission events. 2.) The audience will understand the process by which medical hazards are identified and mitigated before launch. 3.) The audience will understand the roles and responsibilities of all the other flight control positions in participating in the process of reducing hazards and reducing medical risk to an acceptable level.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","meetings":[{"submissionId":20060010432,"name":"Aerospace Medicine Association","location":"United States","id":"d86cd46e15fc4a2ba393bf795564b514"},{"country":"United States","submissionId":20060010432,"endDate":"2005-05-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"Aerospace Medicine Association","location":"United States"}},"meetingId":"315b675b463042458258542bf57ce051","id":"0212dd4d235542edaea95bea56087917"}],"name":"Aerospace Medicine Association Annual Conference","location":"Kansas City, MO","id":"315b675b463042458258542bf57ce051","startDate":"2005-05-08T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20060010432,"id":"7779d8bc5bcc4ae2879517789ceb3538","publicationDate":"2005-01-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[],"downloadsAvailable":false,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":112.022705},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"OTHER","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20150008684,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"aaa4a1aef5834da9af9172100293f342","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Lunar And Planetary Science And Exploration"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20150008684,"ear":"NO","id":"104e6214b6934e92970bbeb4a04779d9","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["AAS 13-712","Report Number: AAS 13-712"],"title":"GRAIL TCM-5 Go/No-Go: Developing Lunar Orbit Insertion Criteria","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PAPER","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2015-05-21T17:18:11.4500000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20150008684,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Chung, Min-Kun J."},"organization":{"name":"Jet Propulsion Lab., California Inst. of Tech.","location":"Pasadena, CA, United States"}},"id":"71713022d2bd4b82ad0dd69a1b7b042a"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20150008684,"sourceIdentifiers":[{"number":"hdl:2014/44903","submissionId":20150008684,"id":"8e4067b3a2624c0c819d0c8d5a5dcf61","type":"HANDLE"},{"submissionId":20150008684,"id":"446f765bb2c3474580093e1dfc77518e","descriptor":"hdl:2014/44903"}],"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2015-05-21T17:18:11.4500000+00:00","center":{"code":"JPL","name":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory","id":"efbc9c123a0f47caafa95d99b7d2e1a1"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"The Gravity Recovery and Interior Laboratory (GRAIL) mission successfully completed mapping the Moon's gravity field to an unprecedented level. The mission success was critically dependent on the success of the Lunar Orbit Insertion (LOI). It was somewhat unfamiliar as it involved an elliptical approach from a low-energy trans-lunar cruise trajectory via Sun-Earth three-body region rather than a more conventional hyperbolic approach from a direct Earth-to-Moon transfer. In addition, how its delivery dispersion affected the science formation of the two spacecraft was not well understood. In this paper we establish a set of LOI criteria to meet all the requirements and we use these criteria to establish Go/No-Go boundaries of the last, statistical Trajectory Correction Maneuvers (TCM-5s) for operations. In the end both spacecraft were found to be within the established boundaries and TCM-5s of both spacecraft were cancelled.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","meetings":[{"submissionId":20150008684,"name":"American Inst. of Aeronautics and Astronautics","location":"Reston, VA, United States","id":"ceee8da43a4e4c55b47d7ee742206caf"},{"submissionId":20150008684,"name":"American Astronomical Society","location":"Washington, DC, United States","id":"b283b927054341c385bc16606b94bfa8"},{"country":"United States","submissionId":20150008684,"endDate":"2013-08-13T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"American Inst. of Aeronautics and Astronautics","location":"Reston, VA, United 